<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Exertional chest tightness and breathlessness for about three months in a 47-year-old heavy smoker who leads a ship-based polar expedition sailing in about four months. Please confirm or exclude obstructive coronary disease, advise on medical therapy and revascularization, and advise whether and how he can safely lead a remote, strenuous, high-latitude expedition. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks, with coronary CT angiography before or on the day of the visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does he have obstructive coronary disease, and what is the best first test?</item><item>Is the heart murmur heard in the past, or a structural problem, part of the picture?</item><item>How should his medical therapy be optimized?</item><item>Does he need revascularization, and what would it offer him?</item><item>Is he fit to lead a remote polar expedition in four months, and what precautions are needed?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Shackleton, a 47-year-old expedition leader, is seen at the request of Dr. Ellingsworth-Daly for three months of exertional chest tightness and breathlessness. Since starting metoprolol two weeks ago the episodes are less frequent but still come two or three times a week on stairs or with a load and pass within three minutes of rest; nitroglycerin has relieved them twice. There is no rest pain, syncope, palpitations, orthopnea, or ankle swelling. He stopped smoking a week ago. He sails in about four months on a long voyage with port calls and weeks at sea far from a hospital, and expects heavy physical work in cold conditions. He wants to lead the expedition and is frank that he would rather not know of anything that could stop him; we agreed that he would hear everything and make the decisions himself. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Ernest Henry Shackleton was born on February 15, 1874, at Kilkea, County Kildare, Ireland, the son of Dr. Henry Shackleton, of a well-known Quaker family. [D]</item><item>On the southern sledge journey of the Discovery expedition he became unable to pull the sledge because of breathlessness and weakness; the collapse was attributed to scurvy and &quot;a sort of asthma,&quot; and he was invalided home against his will in 1903. [D]</item><item>On the Nimrod expedition (1907 to 1909), its medical officer Eric Marshall recorded in his diary on January 20, 1908, that Shackleton was very unwell after pulling on a rope, and twice heard a pulmonary systolic murmur. [D]</item><item>On the Imperial Trans-Antarctic (Endurance) expedition of 1914 to 1917, of fifty-six men three died in the Antarctic: Mackintosh, Hayward, and Spencer-Smith of the Ross Sea party. No member of the Endurance&apos;s own party died. [D]</item><item>His colleague Dr. James McIlroy later recalled that on one occasion Shackleton would not undress and let him listen to his heart. [D]</item><item>He was a heavy cigarette smoker for most of his life. [D]</item><item>After the war he returned to London. In 1921, with John Quiller Rowett taking on practically the whole financial responsibility, he turned his planned Arctic expedition into the Shackleton-Rowett expedition to the South; it was organized and equipped within three months, its sailing was postponed beyond August 20 by a strike of ships&apos; joiners and other delays, and the Quest left St. Katharine&apos;s Dock in London on September 17, 1921. [D]</item></list><paragraph>5 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

