<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Chronic coronary disease with stable exertional angina (I25.118). His typical exertional angina and risk factors gave an intermediate-to-high pretest probability. Coronary CT angiography is a recommended first test at his age and gives the anatomy. It shows a flow-limiting proximal LAD stenosis (CT-FFR 0.70), which explains the angina, with nonobstructive right coronary disease and normal left ventricular function. Without left main disease or a reduced ejection fraction, revascularization in chronic coronary disease improves angina and quality of life but has not been shown to lower the risk of death or heart attack compared with optimized medical therapy. The first step is therefore a higher beta-blocker dose. If angina persists, angiography and a stent to the LAD are reasonable, by shared decision, with dual antiplatelet therapy for six months after an elective stent. [S]</item><item>Past heart murmur and episodes of exertional breathlessness. There is no murmur today, and the echocardiogram shows normal valves, a normal right heart, and no interatrial shunt, so there is no structural explanation such as an atrial septal defect. The present picture is fully explained by the coronary disease. [S]</item><item>Fitness for a remote polar expedition. If he is free of angina on treatment and a symptom-limited exercise test shows good capacity without ischemia, he can sail with precautions. The main risks are an acute coronary event far from a catheter laboratory, bleeding on dual antiplatelet therapy after injury, and the extra cardiac demand of cold and heavy work. Stopping smoking is the single most important change he can make. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Ellingsworth-Daly&apos;s intake note and results: LDL cholesterol 168 mg/dL, HDL 38, triglycerides 200, total cholesterol 246 mg/dL; HbA1c 5.9%; creatinine 0.98 mg/dL; resting ECG normal. Today: coronary CT angiography with a calcium score of 210, an 80% mixed plaque in the proximal left anterior descending artery with a CT-derived fractional flow reserve of 0.70 distal to it, a 30% plaque in the mid right coronary artery, and no left main disease. Transthoracic echocardiogram: normal left ventricular size and wall motion, LVEF 58%, normal valves, normal right heart, and no interatrial shunt on color Doppler or the agitated-saline study. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Increase metoprolol succinate to 100 mg daily; continue aspirin 81 mg, atorvastatin 80 mg, and sublingual nitroglycerin. [S]</item><item>Lipid panel 4 to 12 weeks after starting the statin; add ezetimibe if LDL cholesterol is 55 mg/dL or higher. [S]</item><item>Review in four weeks: if angina persists, invasive coronary angiography by the radial artery with a drug-eluting stent to the proximal LAD, after a shared decision, then aspirin with clopidogrel for six months and aspirin alone after. [S]</item><item>Cardiac rehabilitation, and supervised exercise before the voyage. [S]</item><item>Continue varenicline with counseling; no smoking at sea. [S]</item><item>Before departure: a symptom-limited exercise ECG; a written cardiac summary and medicines with spares; a defibrillator and emergency cardiac medicines aboard; satellite access to cardiology; an evacuation plan to cardiac centers at the ports; and advice on cold stress. [S]</item><item>No routine surveillance stress testing or imaging afterward; test again only if symptoms change. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Ellingsworth-Daly, who saw him the same day. The results and options were explained to Mr. Shackleton with the images, and he agreed with the plan. As he asked, nothing will be shared with his backers without his consent. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Cardiology review after the primary-care visit in four weeks, to decide on angiography; pre-departure review with the exercise test result. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
